      "content": "International organizations like the UN often fail to protect specific national interests because their decision-making is fundamentally constrained by the sovereignty of member states, particularly those wielding disproportionate economic and military power. Democratic legitimacy is frequently diluted by bureaucratic inertia and the Schmittian veto powers inherent in systems like the UN Security Council, which prioritize collective stability over individual justice. Consequently, the idealistic framework of global governance often collapses when coercion becomes necessary, leaving smaller nations vulnerable to powerful blocs deciding their outcomes.",
